---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "ADX Explained — Average Directional Index";
const description =
  "ADX measures trend strength regardless of direction. How it is built from " +
  "DI+ and DI−, what the conventional levels mean, and why it lags by design.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Technical analysis / ADX">
  <h1>ADX — Average Directional Index</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    ADX answers one question: <strong>is this market trending, or going
    sideways?</strong> It says nothing about which way. A stock collapsing and a
    stock surging can both post an ADX of 40 — a high reading means the move is
    orderly and sustained, not that it is upward.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    That single property is what makes it useful alongside a directional
    indicator, and useless on its own.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>
  <p>
    Wilder's construction has three steps. First, measure directional movement on
    each bar:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">+DM = today's high − yesterday's high   (if positive and larger than −DM)
−DM = yesterday's low − today's low    (if positive and larger than +DM)</div>
  <p>Then smooth each over 14 periods, divide by the smoothed true range, and express as percentages:</p>
  <div class="formula">DI+ = 100 × smoothed(+DM) / ATR
DI− = 100 × smoothed(−DM) / ATR</div>
  <p>Finally, ADX is the smoothed average of how far apart those two lines are:</p>
  <div class="formula">DX  = 100 × |DI+ − DI−| / (DI+ + DI−)
ADX = smoothed average of DX over 14 periods</div>
  <p class="lede">
    Note the absolute value in <span class="c">DX</span>. That is exactly where
    direction gets discarded — only the <em>gap</em> between the two lines
    survives, never its sign.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the level</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>below_20</code><span class="meta">no trend</span></div>
      <p>DI+ and DI− are close together. Price is moving without getting anywhere
        — a range. Trend-following approaches tend to be repeatedly stopped out in
        this condition.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>20_to_25</code><span class="meta">borderline</span></div>
      <p>A trend may be forming. The <a href="/screener/">screener</a>'s default
        threshold of 18 sits just below this, which is a deliberately permissive
        starting point rather than a recommendation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>25_to_50</code><span class="meta">trending</span></div>
      <p>A clear directional move. Check DI+ against DI− to find out which way.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>above_50</code><span class="meta">very strong, and often late</span></div>
      <p>Powerful trends produce these readings — and because ADX is doubly
        smoothed, by the time it is this high the move is usually well advanced.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Direction comes from DI+ and DI−</h2>
  <div class="formula">DI+ above DI−  →  upward pressure dominates
DI− above DI+  →  downward pressure dominates
ADX            →  how decisively, either way</div>
  <p class="lede">
    The conventional reading is ADX above 25 <em>with</em> DI+ above DI− for a
    genuine uptrend. ADX alone is only half the information, which is why quoting
    "ADX is 35" without the DI relationship says nothing about whether the stock
    is rising.
  </p>

  <h2>The lag is structural, not a flaw</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    ADX is smoothed twice — once building DI+ and DI−, again turning DX into ADX.
    That is what makes it stable enough to be useful, and it is also why it
    confirms trends rather than anticipating them. ADX will not tell you a trend
    is starting; it tells you one has been running for a while. Expecting
    otherwise is expecting the wrong thing from the tool.
  </p>

  <h2>On the screener</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The <span class="c">adx</span> column is ADX(14) on daily bars. The default
    condition is ADX ≥ 18, which is looser than the textbook 25 — deliberately, so
    the starting view is not already filtered down to almost nothing. Raise the
    slider to 25 or 30 and watch the count fall; that is the trade-off between
    selectivity and sample size made visible.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>Common misreadings</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>high_adx_means_buy</code><span class="meta">no</span></div>
      <p>High ADX during a sustained decline is a strong <em>downtrend</em>. The
        indicator is direction-blind by construction.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>falling_adx_means_reversal</code><span class="meta">not necessarily</span></div>
      <p>A falling ADX means the trend is losing strength — which often precedes a
        sideways range rather than a reversal.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>comparing_across_stocks</code><span class="meta">reasonable, with care</span></div>
      <p>ADX is normalised by true range, so cross-stock comparison is more
        defensible than with raw price indicators. Volatility regimes still
        differ.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/rsi/">RSI</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/moving-averages/">moving averages</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/volume/">volume and relative volume</a>
  </p>
</Base>
